    <li class="archived-comment" id="comment-1655266115">
      <p class="archived-comment-meta"><strong>JK</strong> &middot; <time datetime="2014-10-27T02:33:55Z">October 27, 2014</time></p>
      <div class="archived-comment-body"><p>Great minds, and all that. I've been trying something similar in Trello over the last month. Curious if you have a mental model of how you're tracking "things from my personal kanban system that I've moved into a more public system for work like JIRA or etc."? Obv. can just include a link to the relevant external conversations, but it's bugging me slightly that I've got duplicate data. ... And, I may have just come up with the cleverest solution of all. If it works, I'll get back to you.</p></div>
      <ol class="archived-comment-replies">
        <li class="archived-comment" id="comment-1656914930">
          <p class="archived-comment-meta"><strong>jmcgarr</strong> &middot; <time datetime="2014-10-28T02:11:16Z">October 28, 2014</time></p>
          <div class="archived-comment-body"><p>Dude, glad to hear you've settled on something similar as well.  Trello is hard to beat for raw simplicity.</p><p>Regarding your question, I deal with the duplication (for now).  I am "lucky" enough that I don't have too many other boards that steal my focus.  When I do though, I duplicate cards and don't link.  This is mainly because the details are less important on my personal kanban board.</p><p>I have looked into building a 'bot' to feed JIRA tickets or Github issues to a Trello backlog.  Trello's REST API (<a href="https://trello.com/docs/)" rel="nofollow ugc noopener" target="_blank">https://trello.com/docs/)</a> would make this easy.  Zapier is another option as well.</p><p>If you solve this problem, definitely let me know.</p></div>
          <ol class="archived-comment-replies">
            <li class="archived-comment" id="comment-1656917175">
              <p class="archived-comment-meta"><strong>JK</strong> &middot; <time datetime="2014-10-28T02:13:30Z">October 28, 2014</time></p>
              <div class="archived-comment-body"><p>So I've become a _huge_ Slack fan; my current hypothesis is that I can use Slack with Trello + other integrations (Github, Travis, JIRA, Drive, etc.) and a Hubot clone to basically act as my personal assistant. I'll let you know how it goes.</p></div>
              <ol class="archived-comment-replies">
                <li class="archived-comment" id="comment-3104335269">
                  <p class="archived-comment-meta"><strong>adrian contreras</strong> &middot; <time datetime="2017-01-17T17:35:14Z">January 17, 2017</time></p>
                  <div class="archived-comment-body"><p>so?</p></div>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </li>
    <li class="archived-comment" id="comment-1656740289">
      <p class="archived-comment-meta"><strong>Brian P</strong> &middot; <time datetime="2014-10-27T23:55:39Z">October 27, 2014</time></p>
      <div class="archived-comment-body"><p>Mike, love Trello and the web/mobile capabilities.  At my new gig, I've built a Kanban board with Rational's RTC which does the job for a small team with a three column backlog/doing/done model.  There are so many work tasks that I decided to segregate and only use Trello for personal.  Works well for me and keeps the process light enough.  Thanks!</p></div>
      <ol class="archived-comment-replies">
        <li class="archived-comment" id="comment-1656916770">
          <p class="archived-comment-meta"><strong>jmcgarr</strong> &middot; <time datetime="2014-10-28T02:13:07Z">October 28, 2014</time></p>
          <div class="archived-comment-body"><p>Thanks Brian.  If you haven't already, pick up Personal Kanban by Jim Benson.  Great intro to Kanban.</p><p>I haven't use Rational's RTC, but I honestly learned to hate Rational's product years ago for their bloat, enterprise targeting and how they missed the mark for developers.  Would love to hear how well it's working for you.</p></div>
          <ol class="archived-comment-replies">
            <li class="archived-comment" id="comment-1675686307">
              <p class="archived-comment-meta"><strong>Brian P</strong> &middot; <time datetime="2014-11-04T13:13:18Z">November 4, 2014</time></p>
              <div class="archived-comment-body"><p>Mike, I read Personal Kanban after that Skype session we had at Bb with Jim Benson. It is a great read.  I ended up standing up a Kanban board in Rational Team Concert for my team.  We process a lot of tickets and use a basic three column configuration (Rational limitation) and design modules for as many swim lanes as we need.  Total out of the box functionality with no customization but we've made it work and it keeps us very organized.  I'd prefer another tool but Rational is what the government (our customer) paid for, so we're using it. :(  I am actually presenting on our configuration to an internal development group today.  Hope you don't mind if I use this blog post as a resource.  Thanks!</p></div>
              <ol class="archived-comment-replies">
                <li class="archived-comment" id="comment-1686108929">
                  <p class="archived-comment-meta"><strong>jmcgarr</strong> &middot; <time datetime="2014-11-10T18:18:17Z">November 10, 2014</time></p>
                  <div class="archived-comment-body"><p>Best of luck Brian...and of course you can use this blog entry!</p></div>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </li>
    <li class="archived-comment" id="comment-1729495806">
      <p class="archived-comment-meta"><strong>Danny Hyun</strong> &middot; <time datetime="2014-12-07T03:10:04Z">December 7, 2014</time></p>
      <div class="archived-comment-body"><p>I love Trello, I'll be giving this a try!</p></div>
    </li>
    <li class="archived-comment" id="comment-1848266080">
      <p class="archived-comment-meta"><strong>drewa</strong> &middot; <time datetime="2015-02-11T19:12:46Z">February 11, 2015</time></p>
      <div class="archived-comment-body"><p>Somewhat related to JK's question, but how are you tracking issues for others that aren't Trello users?  Tags is a possibility but i'd like to have something separate and use tags for it's intended purpose.  Anyone with a clever solution for this?  Best i can come up with is a separate list for the people i'm waiting on the most (top3) and then a general waiting list for others with the persons name as part of the card title....but i don't love it.</p></div>
      <ol class="archived-comment-replies">
        <li class="archived-comment" id="comment-1851182465">
          <p class="archived-comment-meta"><strong>jmcgarr</strong> &middot; <time datetime="2015-02-13T05:06:30Z">February 13, 2015</time></p>
          <div class="archived-comment-body"><p>Thanks for the comment.  To be clear, this is really only used for myself.  Personal Kanban is intended for only one person.</p><p>That being said, the waiting column has worked really well for me.  I don't use tags for people.  I just put their name in the card title...and I limit the amount of stuff I am waiting for.</p></div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </li>
    <li class="archived-comment" id="comment-2145281744">
      <p class="archived-comment-meta"><strong>Pranay</strong> &middot; <time datetime="2015-07-19T16:49:00Z">July 19, 2015</time></p>
      <div class="archived-comment-body"><p>Thanks, great help!</p></div>
    </li>
    <li class="archived-comment" id="comment-2160150987">
      <p class="archived-comment-meta"><strong>David Kuster</strong> &middot; <time datetime="2015-07-27T22:15:17Z">July 27, 2015</time></p>
      <div class="archived-comment-body"><p>Mike, just wanted to say thanks for this. I started experimenting with a personal kanban board in Trello when I ran across this post as part of my Netflix interview research, and wanted to provide some feedback as to the process I've settled on.</p><p>I've generally followed the setup of your example board, but have 4 main GTD contexts - Essentialism, Tasks, Tech and Work, with separate backlogs and labels for each. There are 3 additional labels, which are sort of like sub-contexts - House and Financial (sub-contexts of Tasks) and one related to a remote team I manage which is a sub-context of Work. I originally had a Personal backlog as per your example but separated it into Essentialism and Tasks. Essentialism contains things like meditate, exercise, call old friends, etc, and is a bit of a nod to Covey's First Things First. Tasks is mostly the detritus of life - running errands and paying bills and whatnot. Tech is primarily things I want to learn and Work is a little duplicated from JIRA tickets but makes it easier to track things to the level of detail necessary for my needs, as well as whatever work happens outside of JIRA tickets.</p><p>The other change I made is renaming "Priority" to "This Week". I found Priority to be too vague (and too easy to consider too many things a priority), whereas This Week helped make it more concrete. Every Monday morning I spend maybe 30 minutes assessing what needs to happen for the upcoming week, grooming the various backlogs, archiving the done list, and putting cards in This Week. One subsequent win is that for the rest of the week I can generally ignore the backlogs, and primarily just look at the This Week, Today, Doing and Waiting columns. (I also moved Waiting between Doing and Done as if I'm waiting on something, hopefully I've already done my part of it and it's that much closer to going in the Done column.)</p><p>I'd hesitate to use Trello for teams but it's definitely great for a single user and a personal kanban board. I've certainly felt more organized and on top of things since getting into this rhythm. Again, thanks!</p></div>
      <ol class="archived-comment-replies">
        <li class="archived-comment" id="comment-2374934731">
